ARTIFACT ARTIFACTS

Usage: artifact
       artifact roster
       artifact chronicle <name>
       artifact list
       artifact info <item>
       artifact progress
       artifact abilities

Artifacts are unique items of power. Each one exists only once in the world.
Artifacts grow stronger as they are used, rising from level 1 to level 5, and
every bonus they grant scales with that level.

Many artifacts bind themselves to a single owner. A bind-on-pickup artifact
claims you the moment you take it; a bind-on-equip artifact waits until you
first wear it; a bind-on-account artifact can be shared between your own
characters. A bound artifact will not answer to anyone else.

Some artifacts grant an ability you invoke by name, such as SOULSTRIKE,
DIVINEWARD, or DOOMBLAST. Use 'artifact abilities' to see which of yours are
ready.

Artifact weapons may also carry signature strikes that trigger during
combat. Most use the same internal recharge as their other weapon procs, but
true per-hit effects roll independently, and some protect against long streaks
of failed rolls. Use 'artifact info <item>' to see the chance and exact
behavior of a signature strike once you hold the artifact.

Fade, the Shadowblade, has two separate on-hit rolls. Its generic artifact
strike is not its life siphon. The siphon rolls 1-in-16 against living
non-dragon NPCs, deals 40 damage per artifact level through the normal damage
system, and heals 25 percent of the damage actually inflicted. It never heals
beyond your maximum hit points. Use 'artifact info fade' to see both chances.

Doombringer also has two separate on-hit rolls. Its named frenzy rolls 1-in-31
against NPCs and makes one extra main-hand attack per artifact level, up to
five. It has its own 25-second recharge, separate from the generic strike, and
using the frenzy against a good target darkens your alignment by one point.
Use 'artifact info doombringer' to see both chances.

Avernus has an always-checked Bladesong package and a separate named strike.
Bladesong may heal its wielder or pull them out of an ordinary knockdown on
any successful hit. The life transfer rolls 1-in-31 against a living foe,
deals triple damage, and does not consume or wait on the generic artifact-proc
timer. Use 'artifact info avernus' for the exact scaling and healing rules.

Others answer to words. Hold or wear the artifact and use the channel it
listens on: SAY the phrase aloud, WHISPER it to someone, or INVOKE it as a
command. Each artifact decides which. Each such effect recharges on its own
clock, from once an hour to once a week, independently of every other effect
on the same artifact, and those clocks survive a reboot. Use
'artifact info <item>' to see exactly how to speak to an artifact and whether
each of its powers is ready.

Some artifacts grant always-on powers - senses, speed, protections - simply
for being worn. Many of those stay locked until the artifact has grown into
them. 'artifact info <item>' shows both what is active now and what is still
to come.

Temporary artifact powers of the same kind do not stack with each other. A
rage and a battle surge are the same thing happening to you, and the first
one holds until it runs out.

A few artifacts are sworn to a single class and demand real depth in it. Wear
one without that depth and it will burn you every tick you keep it on, it
will not tell you its phrases, and it will not answer when you speak them.

See also: ARTIFACT ROSTER, INVOKE, SOULSTRIKE, DIVINEWARD, DOOMBLAST
#0
ARTIFACT ROSTER ARTIFACT CHRONICLE CHRONICLE ROSTER

Usage: artifact roster
       artifact chronicle <name>

The chronicle is the public record of the artifacts of this world. It lists
every artifact that exists in this campaign and what is currently known of
it:

  unawakened   nobody has ever claimed it, and its name is not yet spoken
  unclaimed    it has been found before, and is free again
  held         someone is carrying it
  lost         it is owned, but its bearer is not in play
  recoverable  it is owned on record and no longer anywhere in the world

The chronicle is deliberately incomplete. An artifact nobody has ever found
is listed only as a rumour, without its name. How an artifact is found stays
out of the record until somebody has actually done it and let the artifact go
again. Bearers are named only when that particular artifact's story is a
public one; otherwise the chronicle names the first bearer instead, which is
history and cannot be used to find anyone.

The chronicle never records where an artifact is. It is a rumour board, not a
treasure map.

'artifact chronicle <name>' gives the fuller entry for one artifact you have
heard of: its lore, how it is come by, and its custody history - when it was
first claimed, how often it has changed hands, and whether the gods have ever
had to reach into the world and pull it back out.

See also: ARTIFACT
#0
INVOKE

Usage: invoke
       invoke <word>

A few artifacts do not answer to ordinary speech at all. They answer to a
command, given deliberately.

Typed on its own, 'invoke' lists every such word that something you are
carrying will answer to. With a word, it speaks that word to whatever is
listening.

Other artifacts listen on other channels: most answer to a phrase SAID aloud,
and some to one WHISPERED. Use 'artifact info <item>' to see which channel a
particular artifact uses. An artifact only ever answers on its own channel.

See also: ARTIFACT
#0
SOULSTRIKE DIVINEWARD DOOMBLAST

Usage: soulstrike <target>
       divineward
       doomblast

These are artifact abilities. Each one only works while you have the artifact
that grants it equipped, and each costs psionic energy and has its own
cooldown.

SOULSTRIKE hurls a bolt of soul energy at a single target. DIVINEWARD wraps
you in a sanctuary of divine light. DOOMBLAST unleashes a wave of doom on
hostile creatures in the room.

Using an artifact ability also feeds experience to the artifact itself.

Artifact abilities are separate from an artifact's called effects, which are
invoked by speaking a phrase on the artifact's own channel rather than by
typing a command. Ability cooldowns and called-effect recharges both survive
a reboot.

See also: ARTIFACT, INVOKE
